To end a C program, you’ve got a few lovely little options—like choosing how to finish a batch of cookies! 🍪

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    /* your code here */

    return 0;
}
```

- `return 0;` from `main` is the standard, tidy way to say “everything baked successfully.”
- `return 1;` (or another nonzero value) typically signals an error.

You can also use `exit()`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

Or, for an error:

```c
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
```

`exit()` is especially useful when you need to stop from inside a helper function rather than returning all the way through `main`.

One important crumb: avoid using `abort()` unless you want to terminate immediately due to a serious, unrecoverable problem—it usually ends the program abnormally and may produce a crash report.
